{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790265298,"nonce":"b5HwmzgUDtIvesF3tqSVoOLaJyU=","title":"Python: map(obj.attr, gen()) drains the generator before reading the callable","message":"Reproducer (main dea6d08b, ZYPY_LLVM=1):\n\n    def f(x):\n        return x + 1\n    def g(x):\n        return x * 10\n    class Box:\n        pass\n    b = Box()\n    b.fn = f\n    def gen():\n        yield 1\n        b.fn = g\n        yield 2\n    print(list(map(b.fn, gen())))\n\nCPython reads b.fn first and prints [2, 3]; zypy prints [10, 20], so the generator ran to its end before b.fn was read. Binding the callable first (`h = b.fn; list(map(h, gen()))`) prints [2, 3]. map and filter are eager lists here (632943b), but the callable is still the first argument and must be evaluated before the iterable is consumed. The map/filter rewrite leaves an assigned attribute like b.fn on the builtin path, which is where this happens.","files":null}]}